WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
- [Narrator] Thank you to
Finimize for supporting PBS.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.380
- Money is one of the oldest
aspects of human culture,

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.420
likely predating the written word.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.200
It's woven into the fabric of
virtually all human activity,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.560
like food, family, work,
religion, and celebration.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.860
So it's not surprising that
there is a wide variety

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.380
of unique money customs around the world.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.500
- Some of these may seem
odd or superstitious,

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.300
but for those who grow up with them,

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.560
they're just a part of life.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.960
They can be ceremonial traditions,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.360
symbols of prosperity or remembrance,

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.140
or even functional practices
that keep communities afloat.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.820
- But they'll all make you realize

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.620
how important money is to humans,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.000
not just economically,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.220
but psychologically and socially as well.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.720
- And maybe they'll encourage you

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.780
to look at your own weird
money traditions in a new way.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:56.670
(bright music)

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.460
Our first stop is Africa,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.840
Kenya to be specific,

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.360
where there is a long
tradition of coming together

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.700
to help community members who are in need.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.920
"Harambee," a Swahili word

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.540
which means "everyone pulling together,"

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.540
is actually the official
motto of the country.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.680
And yes, that is where the
meme-tastic gorilla got his name.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:17.280
R.I.P.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.520
- The word "harambee" can also refer

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.380
to an organized community event

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.200
that raises funds or help
for a specific cause.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.640
If someone in the community

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.040
is facing a significant
problem or life event,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.760
they can contact an elder
family member or tribal leader,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.260
who will meet with other elders

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.440
to determine whether the
situation warrants a harambee.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.540
If so, the word will go out

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.220
to friends, family, and coworkers,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.800
who will come together
to donate money, time,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.900
and emotional support
to the person in need.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.060
Kind of like an in-person GoFundMe.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.520
- Maybe you're not in financial trouble,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.100
but you still find it
difficult to save up money

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.540
for important life goals.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.500
If so, you might be interested
in the tradition of sou-sou

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.420
from West Africa and the Caribbean.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.020
It's kind of like an informal
group savings account.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.240
A number of people agree

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.920
to deposit a certain amount of money

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.500
into a treasury at regular intervals.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.140
Once the total has reached a set goal,

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.560
the entirety is given to
one member of the sou-sou,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.600
and a new recipient is chosen.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.900
This cycle continues until
everyone has been paid out.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.700
- Though they don't pay any interest

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.920
and you only get back what you put in,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.440
sou-sous offer group accountability,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.260
so they can be helpful to
people who have trouble saving

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.780
or don't have alternate access to a loan.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.640
Similar arrangements exist

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.560
in Central and South American communities,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.660
where they can be known as tandas,

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.180
cundinas, juntas, or pandeiros.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.120
However, these arrangements
typically don't involve

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.160
a formal contract or any regulation,

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.540
so you have to be sure

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.700
that you're saving with
people you really trust.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.000
- One of the biggest community
events in any culture

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.020
is the wedding,

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.520
so it's not surprising

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.460
that there are a lot of wedding
customs that involve cash.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.140
In Nigerian weddings,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.700
as the couple hits the dance floor,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.120
guests circle around, showering
money over their heads.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.500
In Filipino culture,

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.320
the women attendees line
up in front of the groom

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.000
to pin money on him,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.040
while the men do the same for the bride.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.040
And Italian brides
sometimes wear a satin bag

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.900
for guests to drop money in.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.620
- In Japan, the bride and
groom receive cash gifts,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.060
known as goshugi,

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.180
in a special envelope
called goshugi-bukuro,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.520
which says how much and from whom.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.280
But you can't just put
cash from your wallet

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:33.780
into the goshugi-bukuro.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.360
The bills must be new and crisp.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.000
- Most of these customs involve generosity

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.400
within a family or tight-knit community,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.660
but many cultural traditions
look to expand that to charity.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.320
One of the five pillars of Islam is zakat,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.880
which means to purify,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.240
and it's essentially a call
to donate money to the needy.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.340
How much and how often can differ,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.900
but it's usually around
2.5% of one's total wealth.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:02.880
- In most Muslim countries,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.700
zakat is voluntary and up to the donor

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.380
to decide who the recipient should be.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.680
But in others, like Pakistan,
Libya, and Malaysia,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.980
zakat is mandatory and collected
by state representatives,

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.480
essentially like a tax.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.940
It functions not just
to help those in need,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.940
but to reinforce the
spirituality of the giver.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.640
By donating a small
percentage of your wealth,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.500
you purify the rest of it.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.140
- In Turkey, one way to fulfill zakat

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:31.620
is with a practice called askida
ekmek, or "hanging bread."

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.120
When you go to the bakery,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.220
you can pay for an extra loaf,

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.260
which the vendor is supposed to set aside

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.060
for whoever can't afford to buy one.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.120
The act of charity is
considered to be more powerful,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.680
because the donor is anonymous.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.900
- The Italians have a similar custom

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.260
known as caffe sospeso,
or "suspended coffee,"

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.680
which originated in the
working-class cafes of Naples.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.800
If someone had a good day,
they can pay it forward

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.500
with an anonymous free
coffee to an unknown person.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.660
You may have seen this practice pop up

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.400
in some American coffee shops.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.360
- Superstition and money go
hand in hand in all cultures.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.900
In China, cash given to children
during the Lunar New Year

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.700
shouldn't contain the number four,

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.340
as it sounds a lot like "death" in Chinese

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.320
and is, therefore, considered unlucky.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.420
The Chinese also have a tradition

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.640
of burning fake money at funerals,

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.080
known as Joss paper,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.120
so that the souls of the deceased

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.040
will have good fortune in the afterlife.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.640
- In Turkey, it's said that
sleeping with gold in your hand

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.500
will bring you wealth and success.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.860
In Greece, an empty wallet
is considered bad luck,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.900
so be sure to put a bill or two inside

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:38.640
before giving one as a gift.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.320
At Christmastime in England,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.080
puddings are often baked
with a coin in the mixture,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.220
bringing good fortune

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.140
to whoever gets the piece
with the coin in it.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:47.640
And, of course, here in America,

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.340
if you see a penny on the
street and pick it up,

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.260
all day long, you'll have good luck.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.300
- You know, the more we talk about

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:55.920
these different money customs,

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.780
the more similar they feel.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.740
They all seem to relate to
family, community, charity,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:02.670
or good fortune.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:05.580
- Yeah, even though money
is a very functional tool,

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.520
you can see there's a lot of
human emotion tied up in it.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.220
It's a symbol for prosperity and happiness

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.660
and also the glue

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.640
that holds families and
communities together.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.380
- Oh, I've got one more

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.420
really weird money tradition for you.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:21.180
Apparently in some Western countries,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.640
they believe in a magical creature

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:27.720
who pays real money for,
get this, children's teeth.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:28.650
- What?

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:29.880
- Not a joke.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.860
When the child loses a tooth,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.840
they put it under their
pillow while they're sleeping,

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.420
and then this elf or goblin or whatever

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.660
sneaks in, takes the tooth,
and leaves money behind.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:41.520
- Ew, what does it do
with all those teeth?

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:42.480
- I don't know.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.900
Maybe that's the currency in
Nightmare Land or something.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:46.890
- Super creepy.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:47.571
- Yeah

00:06:47.571 --> 00:06:49.620
- [Both] And that's our "Two Cents."

